<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Pangaion</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pangaeus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Pangaion is a mountain range in eastern Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> river and the plain of Drama. In antiquity its gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines drew Thasian settlers to the facing coast and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to found Amphipolis in 437 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> seized the mines in the 350s BCE, and their output paid for his army and coinage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-pangaion.jpg" alt="Mount Pangaion" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marsyas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pangaion_from_Philippi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
